WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.780
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar and you're watching Mind Your Decisions.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.220
Consider a number line that goes from zero to ten.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.440
A ball is on the line and starts at position x = 1 meter.

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:18.179
The velocity of the ball is the same as its x position.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.900
So at x = 5.234 meters, its velocity would be 5.234 meters per second (m/s).

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:30.449
How long would it take for the ball to reach x = 10 meters?

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:35.639
I thank Kaan and Fatih Ulupinar for suggesting this problem.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.320
Can you figure it out?

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.320
Give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for a solution.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:58.380
So, how can we solve this problem?

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.929
Well, it's an example of a separable differential equation.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:06.790
The balls velocity at time t equals its position at t.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.930
Translated into the language of differential equations:

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.449
We have the ball's velocity, which is dx/dt, is equal to its position x.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:24.400
Now this is called "separable" because we can separate the x terms on one side and the t terms on the other.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:28.809
So we have dx/x is equal to dt.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.129
We can now integrate both sides of this equation,

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.800
and we get that the natural log of the absolute value of x

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.700
is equal to t plus an arbitrary constant.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.600
(We group all the constants on the right hand side.)

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.600
Now we'll solve for the constant.

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:54.069
We have the initial condition that when the ball starts out at t = 0, its position x is, x = 1.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:58.989
So we substitute in those values and we can solve that C is equal to 0.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:05.140
So, we have the natural log of the absolute value of x is equal to t,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.200
and that'll be the equation that we'll use to solve this problem.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.600
What is the time when x = 10?

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:18.729
Well, we substitute in x = 10, and its time will be the natural log of 10,

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.749
which is approximately 2.3 seconds, and that's our answer!

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.660
Did you figure it out?

